          "id": "e0a8ed4cdffb275b9190efc0a439095a53a0ad42",
          "message": "feat(sparq-trust): delegation invocation-binding gate — non-replayable admitted delegations (sq-l5og) [OPUS-4.8] (#1026)\n\n* feat(sparq-trust): delegation invocation-binding gate — non-replayable admitted delegations (sq-l5og) [OPUS-4.8]\n\nCloses the §4 open security problem of research/solid-trust-graph-authz-design.md:\nan admitted delegation was a standing graph fact ANY session reaching it could ride\n(replay / re-delegation escalation, same severity class as the §2.4 boundary re-opening).\n\nSpecifies + enforces + tests the missing rule (the delegation analogue of §3.4 holder\nbinding): authenticated invoker == the carried chain's terminal delegate, key-proven per\nrequest. New default-OFF `delegation` module in the opt-in `sparq-trust` crate:\n\n- `invoke()` gates a carried ZCAP/UCAN-style chain on: trust-anchored root, a CHECKED\n  delegator signature per hop over a domain-separated `hop_message`, monotone attenuation\n  (child ⊆ parent actions + expiry), terminal-hop expiry vs now, scope, and the two-leg\n  invocation binding — invoker == terminal delegate AND a per-request fresh-challenge\n  proof-of-possession under the terminal key (DPoP/GNAP-style, on the shipped sparq-zk\n  holder_pop_message / sign_holder_pop primitive; no new crypto).\n- The fresh-challenge PoP makes an admitted delegation non-replayable: a captured chain\n  carries no signing key, so it cannot sign a NEW challenge; a session that merely reaches\n  the delegation is not the terminal delegate.\n- Takes the object-capability side of the §4.1↔§4.3 ambient-authority tension (item M):\n  the chain is carried WITH the invocation, never an ambient graph lookup.\n- effective_against_current() intersects against the CURRENT delegator grant (item N),\n  never a delegation-time snapshot — no stale-snapshot escalation after revocation.\n\nAdds the delegation-replay adversarial forgery matrix (analogue of\nacp_forged_*_in_acr_document_does_not_grant): third-party replay, stolen-chain-without-key,\nreplayed-PoP-over-old-challenge, forged/lifted hop signature, escalating attenuation,\nbroken link, expired, out-of-scope — all DENY.\n\nDocuments the design choices (obj-cap model + intersection-binds-current) in the design\nrecord (§4.1, §7.4 K′/M, §7.5) and the crate README; a gh issue flags them for the\nmaintainer to steer. Research PoC, NOT a shipped security guarantee; residual open work\n(deep-chain incremental revocation, DID-resolver delegate-key binding) documented, not solved.\n\nGates: clippy --all-targets in default AND --all-features (-D warnings), fmt, rustdoc\n--all-features (-D warnings), check-readme-template, check-privacy-claims, markdownlint\nall green; sparq-solid builds with trust-graph OFF and ON; 17 delegation tests + 11\nexisting trust tests pass. Written while Fable unavailable — flag for re-review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sparq-trust): bind delegate_key into hop_message — close the key-substitution replay BYPASS (sq-l5og) [OPUS-4.8]\n\nAn adversarial review proved a cryptographic soundness BYPASS in the delegation\ninvocation-binding gate: `hop_message` excluded `delegate_key`, so the terminal\nhop's `delegate_key` was attested by NO signature. An attacker could capture the\nchain off the wire, substitute its OWN public key as `terminal.delegate_key` (the\ngenuine WebID + the genuine delegator signature still verified, because the signed\npreimage excluded the key), set `inv.agent` to the genuine delegate's WebID, and\nsign the fresh challenge with its own key — `invoke()` then CONFERRED the\ncapability. This is exactly the stolen-chain replay the PR claimed to defeat.\n\nFix:\n- hop_message now folds each hop's `delegator_key` AND `delegate_key` into the\n  domain-separated Poseidon2 preimage (via `sparq_zk::sig::holder_key_digest`, no\n  new crypto), so the delegator's signature ATTESTS which key the delegate holds.\n  A substituted terminal key now breaks the delegator's signature -> the chain is\n  rejected at step 2 (HopSignatureInvalid). Holds for ALL hops incl. terminal.\n  The identity key (no affine coords) folds to a distinct sentinel; the Schnorr\n  layer rejects it independently -> fail-closed. This changes the signed preimage\n  (documented as a wire-format/back-compat note in the rustdoc).\n- New negative regression tests in the delegation_replay matrix:\n  `stolen_chain_with_substituted_terminal_key_is_denied` (the exact exploit) and\n  `substituted_terminal_key_on_a_single_hop_chain_is_denied`. Verified they FAIL\n  without the fix (capability conferred to attacker) and PASS with it.\n- Honesty fix: README + delegation.rs docs + the design record + access-control\n  SKILL.md no longer overclaim full non-replayability. They now state the gate\n  defeats stolen-chain KEY-SUBSTITUTION replay, while the upstream key-trust\n  (delegator's own key) is still the open operator-asserted gap (sq-pfae.3,\n  the DID-resolver forgery vector D').\n\nGates: clippy default + --all-features -D warnings (sparq-trust + sparq-solid);\nsparq-solid builds trust-graph OFF and tests trust-graph ON; full sparq-trust\ntests green (19 in delegation_replay); rustdoc --all-features -D warnings clean;\nprivacy-claims clean; readme-template 0 deviations (README 120 lines); typos clean.\n\nWritten while Fable unavailable — flag for re-review when Fable returns.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Wright <jmwright.045@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
